\section{SSH connection reuse}{

Each call to \code{htc_upload()}, \code{htc_submit()}, \code{htc_status()}, or
\code{htc_download()} opens a new SSH connection to the submit server,
which triggers a Duo MFA prompt each time. You can avoid this by
configuring SSH connection reuse (ControlMaster) in your
\verb{~/.ssh/config} file. Add the following block:

\if{html}{\out{<div class="sourceCode">}}\preformatted{Host *.chtc.wisc.edu
  ControlMaster auto
  ControlPersist 2h
  ControlPath ~/.ssh/connections/\%r@\%h:\%p
}\if{html}{\out{</div>}}

Then create the connections directory:

\if{html}{\out{<div class="sourceCode bash">}}\preformatted{mkdir -p ~/.ssh/connections
}\if{html}{\out{</div>}}

After this, only the first connection in a two-hour window will
require Duo authentication. Full documentation:
\url{https://chtc.cs.wisc.edu/uw-research-computing/configure-ssh}
}

\section{Project configuration}{

\code{project_config} is how \code{submitr} learns the layout a \code{toolero} project
already settled on, instead of retyping it. \code{toolero::init_project()}
writes \verb{_toolero.yml} to a project's root once it has resolved the
folder set, and that file records two things: \code{folders}, the full list
of folders the project uses, and \code{conventions}, the names the family
resolves rather than assumes (\code{output_dir}, \code{script_dir}, \code{split_dir}).

Passing \code{project_config = "_toolero.yml"} reads that file once and
returns it under \code{config$project}:

\if{html}{\out{<div class="sourceCode r">}}\preformatted{cfg <- htc_config(project_config = "_toolero.yml")
cfg$project$conventions$output_dir
#> [1] "output"
}\if{html}{\out{</div>}}

As of this release (S-G5), passing the returned config's \code{project}
element on to \code{\link[=htc_gen_executable]{htc_gen_executable()}} and \code{\link[=htc_gen_submit]{htc_gen_submit()}} via their own
\code{config} argument lets \code{results_folder} and \code{queue_from} default from
\code{conventions$output_dir} and \code{conventions$split_dir}, the way
\code{containr::generate_dockerfile()} already reads the same file for its own
purposes:

\if{html}{\out{<div class="sourceCode r">}}\preformatted{cfg <- htc_config(project_config = "_toolero.yml")
htc_gen_executable(r_script = "R/analysis.R", config = cfg)
htc_gen_submit(mode = "multiple", config = cfg)
}\if{html}{\out{</div>}}

\code{r_script} itself is not defaulted from \code{conventions$script_dir}: the
convention names a directory, not a file, and the script's own filename
is project-specific information \verb{_toolero.yml} has no way to record.
}

\section{Security}{

\code{htc.cfg} contains your username and server address. Neither is
sensitive on its own, but \code{htc_config()} adds \code{htc.cfg} to
\code{.gitignore} on creation to avoid accidentally committing
institutional account details to a public repository.
}

